course: biology
lesson: 64
title: Cells, bones and muscles
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Your body is built of cells, tiny living units far too small to see, about 30 trillion of them, each doing its job like workers in a huge city.
explain: Scrape your knee in the playground and watch it over a week: skin cells around the cut divide again and again and crawl across the wound under the scab until new skin covers it.
explain: Inside you stands a frame of 206 bones, and they are not dry and dead like a museum skeleton: living bone is wet, pinkish, full of blood vessels, and grows and mends itself.
explain: Bones cannot move on their own; muscles pull on them, always pulling and never pushing, so they work in pairs, one to bend a joint and one to straighten it.
explain: Some muscles obey you, like the ones you use for star jumps, and some work by themselves day and night, like your heart.
fact: Every living thing is made of cells; under a microscope, cheek cells look like flat shapes with a dark dot in the middle, the nucleus, which holds the cell's instructions.
fact: The human body has about 30 trillion cells of many kinds, such as skin, bone, muscle, nerve and blood cells.
fact: A cut heals because skin cells around it divide and make new cells that crawl across the wound, while a scab protects it from germs.
fact: An adult skeleton has 206 bones; a newborn baby has around 270 to 300, and some of them join together as the child grows.
fact: The smallest bone in the body is the stirrup bone in the middle ear, about 3 millimetres long, and the longest is the thigh bone, the femur.
fact: A smile is made by face muscles, mainly the zygomaticus muscles, which pull the corners of the mouth up and out; more than half of all your bones are in your hands and feet.
fact: Bones show white on an X-ray because the calcium in them stops X-rays more than soft muscle and skin, and a break shows as a dark line; bone cells slowly rebuild a break over several weeks, so a broken arm is kept still in a cast.
fact: Living bone is wet and full of blood vessels and nerves, and the soft marrow inside many bones makes millions of new blood cells every second.
fact: Joints are where bones meet; the knee and elbow are hinge joints that bend one way like a door, while the shoulder and hip are ball-and-socket joints that turn many ways.
fact: Smooth cartilage and a slippery fluid let the ends of bones glide in a joint.
fact: Muscles move bones by pulling, never pushing; the biceps on the front of the arm shortens to bend the elbow, and the triceps at the back pulls to straighten it.
fact: The body has over 600 muscles; heart muscle and gut muscles work by themselves, while arm and leg muscles move only when the brain tells them to.
fact: Sore muscles a day after hard exercise come from tiny tears in muscle fibres that the body repairs, making the muscle a little stronger; it is not caused by acid left in the muscle.
fact: You are about 1 centimetre taller in the morning than at bedtime, because the soft discs between the bones of your spine are squashed during the day and fill up again at night; with no gravity squashing them, astronauts in space grow a few centimetres taller.
exercise: What are all living things made of? | Cells | Bones only | Water only | Tiny stones
exercise: What is the dark dot in the middle of a cheek cell? | The nucleus, which holds the cell's instructions | A speck of dirt that fell on the glass slide from the air | A tiny bone inside the cell | A drop of blood
exercise: About how many cells does a human body have? | About 30 trillion | About 30 | About 30 thousand | About 30 million
exercise: How does a scraped knee heal? | New skin cells grow and crawl across under the scab | The scab hardens into new skin | The blood dries into skin | The skin is glued by sweat
exercise: How many bones does an adult skeleton have? | 206 | 106 | 350 | 1,000
exercise: Why does a baby have more bones than an adult? | Some of a baby's bones join together as it grows | Adults lose bones when they fall over | Babies grow extra bones that later drop out, like milk teeth | Adults' bones dissolve into muscle
exercise: Where is the smallest bone in the body? | In the middle ear | In the tip of the little toe | In the tip of the nose | In the little finger
exercise: What happens in your face when you crack a big smile at a joke? | Face muscles pull the corners of your mouth up and out | Your cheekbones bend upwards a little | Your skin stretches up all by itself, with no muscles needed | Your teeth push your lips apart
exercise: Why are bones white on an X-ray? | The calcium in them stops X-rays more than muscle and skin | Bones are painted white by the hospital before the X-ray picture | Bones glow when X-rays touch them | Bones are full of white milk
exercise: What does the marrow inside bones make? | New blood cells | New teeth for the jaw | Muscles for the legs | Fat for the skin
exercise: What kind of joint is the knee? | A hinge joint that bends one way | A ball-and-socket joint that turns all ways | A fixed joint that cannot move | A joint with no bones in it
exercise: Which joint lets your arm swing round in a big circle? | The shoulder, a ball-and-socket joint | The elbow, a hinge joint | The knee, a hinge joint | The wrist, a fixed joint that cannot move
exercise: What makes the ends of bones glide smoothly in a joint? | Smooth cartilage and a slippery fluid | Tiny wheels between the bones | Oil from the food we eat, stored in the joint | Air bubbles inside the joint
exercise: How do muscles move bones? | By pulling, never pushing | By pushing them, never pulling | By spinning the bones round | By melting and setting again
exercise: Which muscle bends your elbow when you lift a schoolbag? | The biceps on the front of the arm | The triceps at the back | The heart muscle | The muscle in the thigh
exercise: Which of these muscles works without you deciding? | The heart muscle | The biceps in your arm | The muscles you jump with | The muscles you wave with
exercise: Why are your legs sore the day after a long hike? | Tiny tears in muscle fibres are being repaired | Acid has been left inside the muscles and stays there for days | The bones have grown too long | The blood has stopped flowing to them
exercise: Why are you a little taller in the morning? | The discs between your spine bones fill up again overnight | Your bones grow a whole centimetre every night while you sleep | Your muscles stretch while you dream | Your feet swell up in bed
test: my mate says skeletons are just dead stuff inside u like a statue. true? | No, living bone is wet, full of blood vessels and makes blood cells | Yes, bones are dead and dry from birth, like the stones in a wall | Yes, but they come alive at night | No, bones are made of muscle
test: In a cartoon a skeleton walks around with no muscles at all. Why could a real one never move? | Bones can only move when muscles pull on them | Real skeletons are far too heavy to walk on their own | Skeletons need batteries to walk | Real skeletons have too few bones
test: What would happen if your elbow were a ball-and-socket joint instead of a hinge? | Your forearm could swing in many directions instead of one | You could not bend your arm at all, not even to eat your dinner | Your arm would be much shorter | Nothing would change at all
test: Ben says the triceps pushes his arm straight. Is he right? | No, the triceps pulls to straighten it; muscles never push | Yes, the triceps is the only muscle in the whole body that pushes | Yes, all arm muscles push | No, the biceps pushes it straight
test: ugh legs are dead after that hike. coach says its lactic acid stuck in them. right? | No, the soreness is tiny muscle tears being repaired | Yes, the acid stays in the legs for days | Yes, and it slowly turns into bone if you don't stretch it out | No, it means your bones are broken
test: An astronaut grows a few centimetres taller in space. Why? | With no gravity squashing them, the discs in the spine stretch out | Space food makes bones grow | The rocket stretches people as it takes off at high speed | The cold of space makes bones longer
test: Mia marks her height at 7 in the morning and at 9 at night. What will she find? | She is about 1 centimetre shorter at night | She is exactly the same height | She is 10 centimetres taller at night than in the morning | She is taller at night after dinner
test: A doctor looks at an X-ray and sees a dark line across a white bone. What does it show? | A break in the bone | A healthy growing bone | A blood vessel in the arm | A spot where the X-ray ran out
test: Why can you still breathe and your heart still beats while you sleep? | Some muscles work by themselves without you deciding | You wake up every minute to work them | You dream about breathing | Muscles store all the beats in the daytime
test: Why does a broken arm need a cast for weeks? | Living bone mends itself slowly and must be kept still | The cast makes new bone out of plaster | Bones never mend, so the cast stays for life | The cast keeps germs out of the bone only
test: In a game a hero's broken bone heals by eating one apple. What really happens? | Bone cells slowly rebuild the break over several weeks | The bone heals in a second with food | Bones never heal once broken | The broken bone is pushed out and a new one grows in its place
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the skeleton of an infant comprises a greater number of bones than that of an adult. | Certain bones fuse together in the course of growth | Adults lose bones through injury | Infants possess additional temporary bones that are shed | The bones of adults are absorbed into muscle
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the action of skeletal muscle upon bone? | Muscle acts only by pulling, often in opposing pairs | Muscle acts exclusively by pushing | Muscle rotates bones by spinning them within their sockets | Muscle acts upon bone only during sleep
exam: It is asserted that bone within the living body is a dry and inert material. Select the response consistent with the established facts. | The assertion is false; living bone is vascular and produces blood cells | The assertion is true of all bones | The assertion is true of the bones of the leg only, those of the arm being living | The assertion is true after the age of ten
exam: Ascertain the cause of the diurnal variation in human stature of approximately one centimetre. | Compression and recovery of the intervertebral discs | The nightly growth of the long bones | The swelling of the feet by evening | The contraction of the heart muscle
